// TASK 5: MACs and Authenticated Encryption
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Adding a Secret to the Seal</h2>

    <p>A plain hash proves data was not altered, but anyone can compute a hash, including an attacker. A Message Authentication Code, or MAC, solves this by requiring a secret key to produce and verify the seal. Only someone holding the key can create or check a valid MAC.</p>

    <p>HMAC is the standard construction for this. It combines a hash function with a secret key in a specific way, giving you authenticity on top of the integrity a plain hash already provides.</p>

    <!-- SVG: hash versus MAC comparison
         Teaching purpose: show the learner that a MAC requires a secret key while a plain hash does not,
         which they would otherwise treat as interchangeable concepts. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 170" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Hash function versus MAC">
            <rect x="10" y="15" width="340" height="140" rx="10" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">Hash Function</text>
            <text x="180" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Data →</text>
            <text x="180" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Digest</text>
            <text x="180" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Anyone can compute this</text>

            <rect x="410" y="15" width="340" height="140" rx="10" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="580" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">MAC (HMAC)</text>
            <text x="580" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Data + 🔑 Secret Key →</text>
            <text x="580" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Tag</text>
            <text x="580" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Only the key holder can compute or verify this</text>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> AEAD Bundles This In</h4>
        <p>Encrypting data without also authenticating it leaves it vulnerable to tampering. This is exactly why AEAD modes like AES-GCM exist, they combine encryption and a MAC-like authentication tag into a single operation.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: Digital Signatures and Common Mistakes
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Proving Who Sent It</h2>

    <p>A digital signature is created using the signer's private key and verified using their public key. Anyone with the public key can confirm the signature is valid, but only the private key holder could have created it.</p>

    <p>This gives signatures a property MACs cannot provide: non-repudiation. With a MAC, either party holding the shared secret could have generated it, so you cannot prove which one did. With a signature, only one party holds the private key, so the signer cannot credibly deny having signed it.</p>

    <p>Understanding the primitives is only half the job. Most real-world cryptographic failures come from implementation mistakes rather than broken math. A few show up constantly in production systems.</p>

    <h3>Common Implementation Mistakes</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Mistake</th>
                <th>Why It's Dangerous</th>
                <th>Correct Approach</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>ECB mode</strong></td>
                <td>Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks, leaking structure and patterns</td>
                <td>Use an AEAD mode such as AES-GCM</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Static IV</strong></td>
                <td>Reusing an IV with the same key breaks confidentiality guarantees for AEAD ciphers</td>
                <td>Generate a fresh random IV for every encryption operation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Custom algorithm</strong></td>
                <td>Home grown cryptography almost always contains subtle, exploitable flaws</td>
                <td>Use vetted, peer reviewed libraries and standards</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Never Roll Your Own Crypto</h4>
        <p>Never design your own cryptographic algorithm. Even experts get this wrong. Use established, peer reviewed libraries and standards instead of building something new.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A surprising number of real-world breaches trace back not to broken math but to exactly these implementation mistakes. Knowing the primitives protects you only if you also avoid the traps around them.</p>
</div>
HTML;

