// TASK 8: Conclusion
$task_contents[8] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>You have now covered the five primitive families that make up applied cryptography: symmetric encryption, asymmetric encryption, hash functions, MACs, and digital signatures. Real systems combine all of them. TLS, for example, uses asymmetric cryptography to exchange a key, symmetric AEAD encryption to protect the bulk of the data, and signatures to prove server identity.</p>

    <p>Understanding the math behind these primitives matters less day to day than avoiding the mistakes that undermine them. ECB mode, static IVs, and home grown algorithms have quietly broken more real systems than any weakness in the underlying cryptography itself.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Symmetric encryption, such as AES-GCM and ChaCha20, provides confidentiality using a shared key.</li>
        <li>Asymmetric encryption, such as RSA and ECC, solves key distribution using public and private key pairs.</li>
        <li>Hash functions provide integrity but are not encryption and are not suitable alone for passwords.</li>
        <li>MACs add authenticity to hashing using a shared secret; AEAD modes bundle this with encryption.</li>
        <li>Digital signatures provide authenticity and non-repudiation using private key signing.</li>
        <li>ECB mode, static IVs, and home grown cryptography are common mistakes that undermine real-world security.</li>
